            {
                "entry_id": "cbd86857-c573-32ab-987a-682ef50ca50f",
                "word": "Midland",
                "pos": "name",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "b1928770-3df8-32a6-89bd-85cbd39c03f4",
                        "entry_id": "cbd86857-c573-32ab-987a-682ef50ca50f",
                        "glosses": [
                            "The loosely-defined region of the United States that is between the North and the South, where Midland American English is spoken."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "6f3612b1-e854-4a46-8a43-d7665816696b",
                                "sense_id": "b1928770-3df8-32a6-89bd-85cbd39c03f4",
                                "text": "Throughout the Midland and the South, the nucleus of /aw/ is well front of center, [...]"
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "309a14d1-aa04-30af-ace1-452f98d16e2a",
                        "entry_id": "cbd86857-c573-32ab-987a-682ef50ca50f",
                        "glosses": [
                            "The English Midlands."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "attributive"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "165172de-2101-406c-ac64-48175c98bd72",
                                "sense_id": "309a14d1-aa04-30af-ace1-452f98d16e2a",
                                "text": "[...] the difficult Southern English found in the Kentish Ayenbite of Inwyt, or even from the Midland of Chaucer's poems."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "a8402c45-9486-4724-a4ed-d1f6c712df8d",
                                "sense_id": "309a14d1-aa04-30af-ace1-452f98d16e2a",
                                "text": "The striking positional variants [...] of Virginia are probably an American innovation, since this feature is not found in Standard British English or the folk speech of the Midland and the south of England, though northern England and Scotland have it to some extent.\n[…]\nIt is worth noting that only two instances of the pronunciation /jɛst/, riming with guest, have been recorded in the New England settlement area (one near Boston, the other near Buffalo), a pronunciation widely used in the English Midland along with /jɪst/, riming with fist. These are the normal derivatives of OE gist, gest..."
                            }
                        ]
